ity breath of the wild  had when it came to its story  although being a very different game i  think it definitely could have used  cutscenes of this caliber  and i genuinely hope that nintendo will  carry this part of age of calamity  through in breath of the wild too  i mean can you imagine ganondorf with a  voice on par  or even better than that of koga suga  and astor  it would be magical but enough about  that it's time to talk about the actual  narrative  now i'm not gonna lie when i first  finished the main campaign  i felt like the story was a total  cop-out as someone who's been looking  forward to seeing the story of the  calamity play out ever since the  announcement of the game  yeah i really felt like they danced  around the brutal aspects of the event  in retrospect however the story has  managed to grow on me over time and in  hindsight i get why nintendo chose to go  this route with the narrative  now you might think i'm just fanboying  here and being biased by giving the  story a pass but i can assure you that  this is not the case  i mean i literally made an entire series  illustrating how the events of the  calamity played out and predicting how  and where they could add gameplay  sections  if there's anyone who wanted to see the  true story of 100 years ago  it's me but i'm also reasonable enough  to realize that we may have just been  a bit too naive thinking they would  actually depict a horrific event like  this in a zelda game  spin-off or not this is still a nintendo  game we're talking about and the actual  events of the calamity as we know it in  breath of the wild is just  really really dark we're talking  genocide here  townsend civilians getting obliterated  by the thousands with no help of  salvation  it's one thing to recount a story like  this and another to  actually show it and aside from that i  can also understand that they may have  ran into some massive hurdles in terms  of the gameplay  how do you make an enjoyable game where 